  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nSummer-like temperatures will persist into early next week as a \nstubborn upper-level ridge sits across the eastern CONUS. For most, \nhighs today will climb into the low-mid 80s in the wake of a \nnorthward moving surface boundary. The exception is across the NE \nCWA, where temperatures will be a few degrees cooler in the upper \n70s with a more southeasterly flow. As has been widely advertised, \nthis is 10-20 degrees above average for this time of the year, near \nthe 90th percentile (normal high for today is 71F in Kansas City, \n69F in Kirksville, and 71F in Sedalia.) \n\nOvernight, a weak surface low will move NE across central Nebraska. \nAscent associated with the low along with the nose of a\nstrengthening low-level jet across Iowa will be enough to \ninitiate nocturnal showers and thunderstorms across portions of \nIowa, but it's unlikely (<10%) that this activity will propagate\ninto northern Missouri. Forecast soundings continue to show a \ndeep dry layer below 600mb, so the required moisture isn't in \nplace to support precipitation.\n\nTemperatures over the weekend will range from the low 80s across \nnorthern Missouri to the low 90s in central Missouri as the upper-\nlevel ridge maintains its position over the area. This ridge and a \ndeveloping western trough will keep the remnants of Hurricane Isaias \ngenerally east of the Mississippi River heading into next week, with \nno sensible effects forecast across our area. \n\nConsiderable uncertainty is introduced Tuesday through Thursday as \nthe trough over the Rockies begins to dig southeast. Moisture from \nanother landfalling tropical storm (Simon) on the west coast of \nMexico will work its way north into the south-central Plains, \nbringing increased PoPs with it. The speed at which the trough moves \ninto the Plains will have a major impact on the mid-week forecast. A \nslower solution (favored by ~60% of LREF ENS members and the \ndeterministic AIGFS) would push rain chances back to the Wednesday \nnight-Friday time frame and keep highs in the upper 80s to low 90s \nthrough midweek. A more progressive solution (favored ~50% of LREF \nGEFS members and the deterministic ECMWF) could bring rain chances \nin as early as Tuesday morning and lower highs down into the mid \n70s. The NBM reflects this uncertainty well with a 10-15 degree \ninterquartile spread on Tuesday and Wednesday.",
